    </technology>


  <exercises xml:id="homework-chp8sec1">
    <title>Problems</title>

    <exercise xml:id="prob-basis-grids">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the following two ordered bases: <m>\mathcal{S}=\left\{ \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}\right\}</m> and <m>\mathcal{B}=\left\{ \begin{bmatrix}2\\1\end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}-1\\3\end{bmatrix}\right\}</m>. Let <m>P_{\mathcal{B}}</m> denote the transformation from <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-coordinates into <m>\mathcal{S}</m>-coordinates.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol label="(a)">
            <li><p>Carefully draw and label the coordinate grids given by both ordered bases. (Make it plenty big because you will be drawing lots of things on it.) You may do this by hand or use GeoGebra.</p></li>
            <li><p>Shade in <m>\vect{S}_\mathcal{B}</m>, the <m>\mathcal{B}</m>-unit square.</p></li>
            <li><p>Let <m>A=P_{\mathcal{B}}\begin{bmatrix}-1\amp 0\\0\amp 2\end{bmatrix}P_{\mathcal{B}}^{-1}</m>. Sketch or use GeoGebra to show how <m>A</m> transforms <m>\vect{S}_\mathcal{B}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Using complete sentences and geometric words, explain how <m>A</m> transforms the plane.</p></li>
            <li><p>Let <m>B=P_{\mathcal{B}}\begin{bmatrix}2\amp 1\\0\amp 2\end{bmatrix}P_{\mathcal{B}}^{-1}</m>. Sketch or use GeoGebra to show how <m>B</m> transforms <m>\vect{S}_\mathcal{B}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Using complete sentences and geometric words, explain how <m>B</m> transforms the plane.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="prob-red-blue-transform">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The matrix <m>A</m> transforms the plane by sending the red region below to the blue region.
        </p>
        <sidebyside widths="40% 55%" valigns="middle middle">
          <p>
            <ol label="(a)">
              <li><p>Using complete sentences and geometric words, <alert>verbally</alert> explain how <m>A</m> transforms the plane.</p></li>
              <li><p>Find the <alert>symbolic</alert> expression for <m>A</m> and give its associated similar matrix decomposition.</p></li>
            </ol>
          </p>
          <image source="images_hw/HW06similartransform.png" />
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="prob-verbal-descriptions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Below is a verbal description of how two matrices transform the plane. For each, (i) give a <alert>symbolic</alert> expression for the described similar matrix decomposition, and (ii) clearly sketch or use GeoGebra to <alert>visually</alert> show how the described matrix transforms the plane, relative to the relevant coordinate system.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol label="(a)">
            <li><p><m>A_1</m> stretches by a factor of 4 in the <m>\vect{v}_1=\begin{bmatrix}2\\-1\end{bmatrix}</m> direction and stretches by a factor of 2 in the <m>\vect{v}_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\\3\end{bmatrix}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>A_2</m> shears the vector <m>\vect{v}_2=\begin{bmatrix}3\\0\end{bmatrix}</m> by 2 units in the direction of the vector <m>\vect{v}_1=\begin{bmatrix}-2\\1\end{bmatrix}</m> and does not move the vector <m>\vect{v}_1</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="prob-matrix-powers">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose we know that <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}4\amp -3\\3\amp 4\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}2\amp 0\\0\amp 1\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}4\amp -3\\3\amp 4\end{bmatrix}^{-1}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol label="(a)">
            <li><p>Recall <m>A^2 = A\cdot A</m>. In complete sentences and geometric word, verbally explain how <m>A^2$</m> transforms the plane. (Hint: Consider using the SMD together with the associative property of matrix multiplication.)</p></li>
            <li><p>Generalize your reason to explain how <m>A^n</m> transforms the plane.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>


</section>